WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.835
Heat spreading along a rod fades as it spreads.

2
00:00:08.115 --> 00:00:12.011
Sampled at grid points, the temperatures form a

3
00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:14.664
vector u that obeys du/dt = −Au.

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.865
The matrix A splits into a dissipative part L,

5
00:00:17.865 --> 00:00:21.172
which is positive semidefinite, and a Hermitian part H.

6
00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:25.041
L drains the norm of the solution, while H alone would conserve it.

7
00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:29.486
A quantum circuit applies unitary operations, which never

8
00:00:29.486 --> 00:00:31.583
change the length of a state vector.

9
00:00:31.863 --> 00:00:36.463
The solution shrinks, so this evolution cannot be a unitary on the system alone.

10
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:42.920
Linear combination of Hamiltonian simulation, or LCHS, writes the decaying

11
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.895
evolution as a weighted integral over a real variable k.

12
00:00:47.175 --> 00:00:51.174
Each k contributes a unitary evolution generated by

13
00:00:51.174 --> 00:00:53.840
kL + H, weighted by a kernel g(k).

14
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.407
For a single decay rate λ, every branch only

15
00:00:57.407 --> 00:01:01.666
rotates, yet the weighted sum shrinks exactly as e^(−λt).

16
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.285
On qubits, the integral becomes a finite quadrature sum.

17
00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:11.444
An ancilla register indexes the quadrature points and controls every Hamiltonian simulation.

18
00:01:11.724 --> 00:01:17.926
For the test problems in this paper, the standard sizing rule at accuracy

19
00:01:17.926 --> 00:01:22.769
0.1 calls for 192 to 632 terms on 8 to 10 ancilla qubits.

20
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.712
The variable k is continuous, and so is

21
00:01:25.712 --> 00:01:28.674
the position of a quantum harmonic oscillator.

22
00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:34.328
The hybrid construction replaces that register with one oscillator mode and

23
00:01:34.328 --> 00:01:37.553
evolves the joint Hamiltonian x̂ ⊗ L + I ⊗ H.

24
00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:41.276
At each position x, the qubits then evolve

25
00:01:41.276 --> 00:01:44.063
under the LCHS unitary with k = x.

26
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.711
This one-mode dilation is shared with Schrödingerisation and qumodisation.

27
00:01:48.991 --> 00:01:54.414
The paper contributes a finite-resource oscillator realization of the LCHS kernel,

28
00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:58.052
with an error and cost analysis for each circuit block.

29
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.916
The kernel enters through two oscillator states, a squeezed vacuum φ and a

30
00:02:03.916 --> 00:02:08.505
prepared state ψ, whose wavefunctions multiply to g(x) up to a constant.

31
00:02:08.785 --> 00:02:13.181
The circuit prepares ψ, runs the joint evolution, undoes the squeezing, and

32
00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:16.697
keeps the runs that find the oscillator in its vacuum state.

33
00:02:16.977 --> 00:02:22.133
This postselection evaluates the LCHS integral and leaves the

34
00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:26.191
qubits in e^(−At)u₀, up to a known scale factor.

35
00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:30.545
A physical oscillator state needs finite energy, so

36
00:02:30.545 --> 00:02:33.001
ψ is built from N squeezed Fock states.

37
00:02:33.281 --> 00:02:38.313
Truncating the ideal kernel state to N terms leaves an error that falls

38
00:02:38.313 --> 00:02:42.140
faster than any power of N, and the paper uses N = 32.

39
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.632
The state has stellar rank 31, a measure of its non-Gaussian resource.

40
00:02:46.912 --> 00:02:53.202
Law–Eberly synthesis prepares it with 31 Jaynes–Cummings pulses and 31 qubit rotations.

41
00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:58.079
Each hybrid term compiles to a displacement of the oscillator

42
00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:01.518
controlled by one qubit, wrapped in qubit basis changes.

43
00:03:01.798 --> 00:03:05.989
A product formula repeats these layers, and the paper bounds the number

44
00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:10.061
of steps in terms of evolution time, accuracy, and oscillator cutoff.

45
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.058
Postselection succeeds in about 10 to 16 percent

46
00:03:14.058 --> 00:03:16.619
of runs for the four-point heat benchmarks.

47
00:03:16.899 --> 00:03:20.046
A perturbation bound ties this success probability to

48
00:03:20.046 --> 00:03:22.361
the total error of the implemented map.

49
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:28.785
Full circuit-level simulations covered heat equations with three boundary conditions,

50
00:03:28.785 --> 00:03:32.578
grids of up to 32 points, and non-normal advection–diffusion.

51
00:03:32.858 --> 00:03:38.990
Across these one-dimensional cases, the fixed-scale map error stayed at or below 1.48 percent.

52
00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:43.413
A four-by-four two-dimensional case, run with parameters chosen

53
00:03:43.413 --> 00:03:46.832
on the one-dimensional family, reached 7.40 percent.

54
00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:51.207
Compared with qubit-only LCHS sized by that rule, one

55
00:03:51.207 --> 00:03:55.816
oscillator with 32 coefficients replaces the ancilla register.

56
00:03:56.096 --> 00:04:00.430
The hybrid route has the smaller fixed-scale error in all ten instances,

57
00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:05.006
while the qubit-only route accepts with fewer repetitions in every instance.

58
00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:11.758
These results support one oscillator mode as an alternative to a discretized ancilla register

59
00:04:11.758 --> 00:04:18.168
when quadrature cost dominates, at the price of oscillator operations and extra postselection repetitions.
